The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
__nfs4_file_get_access(fp, NFS4_SHARE_ACCESS_READ);
if (!fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY]) {
    fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY] = nf;
}
fp->fi_rdeleg_file = nfsd_file_get(fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY]);
```
Reason:
The call to `__nfs4_file_get_access` and the assignment `fp->fi_rdeleg_file = nfsd_file_get(...)` were performed unconditionally. If another thread had already set `fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY]`, the access count and file reference were incremented again unnecessarily, causing an extra reference that prevented the `nfsd_file` from being freed. This extra reference triggered a BUG in `kmem_cache_destroy` on server shutdown.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY]) {
    __nfs4_file_get_access(fp, NFS4_SHARE_ACCESS_READ);
    fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY] = nf;
    fp->fi_rdeleg_file = nfsd_file_get(fp->fi_fds[O_RDONLY]);
    nf = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
By moving `__nfs4_file_get_access` and the `nfsd_file_get` inside the conditional block, they are only executed when the read file descriptor is not already set. This ensures that the access count and file reference are incremented exactly once per file, preventing the reference leak and the subsequent BUG on shutdown. The `nf = NULL` prevents a duplicate put of the file later.

